@page "/learn/wasm-culture-from-server"
@layout LearnLayout

<PageTitle>The same culture in the browser · Field Guide</PageTitle>

<article class="l-article" data-learn>
    <h1 class="l-title">The same culture in the browser</h1>
    <p class="l-sub">The server renders a German invoice. Does it stay German once WebAssembly takes over?</p>
    <p class="l-meta">.NET 11 · WebAssembly · Interactive WebAssembly · about 5 minutes</p>
    <p class="l-new">
        <b>What's new in .NET 11:</b> a prerendered WebAssembly component now uses the same culture the server used.
        In .NET 10 you had to pass the culture along yourself, with a start-up script or a parameter on each
        component.
    </p>

    <p>
        A German customer opens an invoice. The server renders the page in German, so the total reads
        <code>1.234,56</code> and the due date reads <i>Freitag</i>. The invoice is a WebAssembly component. The server
        renders it first (that's "prerendering"), then it starts running in the browser.
    </p>
    <p>
        Here's the code. The page prerenders the invoice, and the invoice formats its numbers the plain way:
    </p>
    <div class="fig">
        <span class="fig-title">The page, and the invoice component</span>
        <pre class="snip">&lt;InvoiceSummary Lines="lines" Due="due" @@rendermode="InteractiveWebAssembly" /&gt;

<span class="dim">// InvoiceSummary.razor</span>
&lt;th&gt;<span class="err">@@Total.ToString("N2")</span>&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Payment due &lt;b&gt;<span class="err">@@Due.ToString("D")</span>&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</pre>
    </div>
    <p>
        The browser has its own language, and it may not be German. Fig. 1 shows what happened on .NET 10 in an English
        browser. It's a recording, because this app runs .NET 11.
    </p>

    <DemoFigure Number="1" Title="The invoice on .NET 10" Url="demo/wasm-culture/invoice/net10"
                Label=".NET 10 · recorded" Side="before">
        <Caption>
            A recording, one row per moment. The server sent <b>1.234,56</b> and <b>Freitag</b>. Once WebAssembly
            started, it used the browser's culture, <b>en-US</b>, and the same invoice said <b>1,234.56</b> and
            <b>Friday</b>. The last row is a workaround: the page passed "de-DE" in as a parameter. WebAssembly still
            ran in en-US, but the total was formatted with the culture passed in, so it stayed German. The date
            wasn't, so it turned English.
        </Caption>
    </DemoFigure>

    <h2><small>The problem</small>Two renders, two cultures</h2>
    <p>A prerendered WebAssembly component renders twice. In .NET 10, each render picked its own culture:</p>
    <div class="fig">
        <span class="fig-title">Where each render got its culture in .NET 10</span>
        <pre class="snip">1. Server prerender   culture from the request <span class="dim">(?culture=de-DE)</span>   <span class="ok">1.234,56</span>
2. WebAssembly starts culture from the browser <span class="dim">(navigator.languages[0])</span>  <span class="err">1,234.56</span></pre>
    </div>
    <p>
        The server never told the browser its culture. So the same <code>ToString("N2")</code> gave German on the
        server and English a moment later. In a French browser you'd get <code>1 234,56</code> and <i>vendredi</i>.
        Whatever the browser speaks wins.
    </p>

    <h2><small>The fix</small>Add localization, keep prerendering</h2>
    <p>
        There's no new API. A localized app already sets up localization in the server's <code>Program.cs</code>, and
        WebAssembly components prerender by default. On .NET 11, that's all it takes:
    </p>
    <div class="fig">
        <span class="fig-title">What turns it on</span>
        <pre class="snip"><span class="dim">// Program.cs (server)</span>
<span class="hl">builder.Services.AddLocalization();</span>
...
app.UseRequestLocalization(...);

<span class="dim">// The page: the component must be prerendered (the default)</span>
&lt;InvoiceSummary <span class="hl">@@rendermode="InteractiveWebAssembly"</span> /&gt;
<span class="dim">// not new InteractiveWebAssemblyRenderMode(prerender: false): no prerender, no culture</span>

<span class="dim">// The component: plain formatting, no culture passed in</span>
&lt;td&gt;@@Total.ToString("N2")&lt;/td&gt;</pre>
    </div>
    <p>Here's an invoice on .NET 11, rendered in German.</p>

    <DemoFigure Number="2" Title="An invoice on .NET 11" Url="demo/wasm-culture/invoice?culture=de-DE"
                Label=".NET 11 · live">
        <Caption>
            It opens in German. When the badge says <b>Running on WebAssembly</b>, the total and the due date are still
            German, whatever your browser's language. Click <b>fr-FR</b> and the page reloads in French.
        </Caption>
    </DemoFigure>

    <p>None of the demo's code mentions the browser's culture. Here are its three files:</p>

    <CodeFigure Number="3" Path="Components/Guide/Demos/WasmCulture/Invoice.razor" Feature="wasm-culture-from-server">
        <Caption>
            The page Fig. 2 runs. Its culture comes from <code>?culture=</code>, and it prerenders the invoice with
            <code>InteractiveWebAssembly</code>.
        </Caption>
    </CodeFigure>

    <CodeFigure Number="4" Path="Client/WasmCulture/InvoiceSummary.razor" Feature="wasm-culture-from-server">
        <Caption>
            The component. It uses plain <code>ToString("N2")</code> and <code>ToString("D")</code>, the same code
            that flipped in Fig. 1, with no culture parameter.
        </Caption>
    </CodeFigure>

    <CodeFigure Number="5" Path="Components/Guide/Demos/WasmCulture/WasmCultureSetup.cs" Feature="wasm-culture-from-server">
        <Caption>
            The setting behind the fix, <code>Components:UseCultureFromServer</code>. This demo sets it to
            <code>"true"</code> to show it, but it's already on because the app calls <code>AddLocalization()</code>.
            You'd only set it yourself to turn the feature off, or to turn it on in an app without
            <code>AddLocalization()</code>.
        </Caption>
    </CodeFigure>

    <h2><small>The .NET 10 way</small>Pass the culture by hand</h2>
    <p>In .NET 10 you had to pass the culture along yourself. These were the two usual ways:</p>
    <div class="fig">
        <span class="fig-title">Two .NET 10 workarounds, and what happened</span>
        <div class="flow">
            <div class="box">
                <pre class="snip">&lt;script src="_framework/blazor.web.js"
        <span class="hl">autostart="false"</span>&gt;&lt;/script&gt;
&lt;script&gt;
  Blazor.start({ webAssembly: {
    <span class="hl">applicationCulture:</span>
      "@@CultureInfo.CurrentCulture.Name" } });
&lt;/script&gt;</pre>
                <span class="why">a hand-written start-up script in App.razor</span>
                <div class="verdict ok">✓ both lines stayed German</div>
            </div>
            <div class="box">
                <pre class="snip">[Parameter] public string? Culture { get; set; }

@@Total.ToString("N2", <span class="hl">culture</span>)
@@Due.ToString("D")  <span class="err">← forgot it</span></pre>
                <span class="why">the culture's name passed to each component, and handed to every format call</span>
                <div class="verdict bad">✗ the total stayed German; the date flipped to the browser's language</div>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
    <p>
        The script works, but it's extra code to repeat what the server already knew. The parameter breaks as soon as
        you miss one format call, as Fig. 1's third row shows.
    </p>

    <h2><small>.NET 11</small>The culture travels with the page</h2>
    <p>
        In .NET 11 the server saves its culture into the page as it prerenders. It saves the UI culture too, which
        picks the language for translated text. WebAssembly reads them
        back as it starts, before any component renders. No script, no parameter: plain formatting gives the same
        result on both sides.
    </p>

    <h2><small>Good to know</small></h2>
    <ul class="gk">
        <li><b>It needs prerendering.</b> With <code>prerender: false</code> there's no server render to copy from, so WebAssembly uses the browser's language.</li>
        <li><b>It's set once, at start.</b> An enhanced navigation (Blazor swapping in the next page without a full load) doesn't change WebAssembly's culture. That's why the demo's culture links reload the whole page.</li>
        <li><b>Language data.</b> To keep downloads small, WebAssembly only loads formatting data for some languages, picked from the browser's language. A Japanese browser, for example, doesn't get German. To load every language, set <code>BlazorWebAssemblyLoadAllGlobalizationData</code> to <code>true</code> in the client project.</li>
        <li><b>To turn it off,</b> set <code>Components:UseCultureFromServer</code> to <code>false</code>. Some older posts describe a property from Preview 5 instead. That property is gone; this setting replaced it.</li>
    </ul>
</article>
